00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.300
Hello, and welcome back to
another video with ItsDrDan.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:07.910
And today we're going to be learning
about the properties of acids and bases.

00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:12.479
Now, acids and bases are some of the most
common chemicals that we use, and not

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.979
only in chemistry, but also in biology.

00:00:15.310 --> 00:00:19.149
So this tends to be one of the more
important types of chemicals that

00:00:19.149 --> 00:00:24.145
you learn about to prepare yourself,
whether it's for chemistry, biology, Um,

00:00:24.175 --> 00:00:28.495
chemical engineering or in the health
sciences, acids and bases are all around

00:00:28.495 --> 00:00:30.575
us, especially in our everyday life.

00:00:31.125 --> 00:00:34.605
So when it comes to acids and bases,
the first thing that's all about is

00:00:34.605 --> 00:00:37.035
exactly how can we classify them?

00:00:37.464 --> 00:00:41.474
Now, it all started around in the late
1800s when a famous scientist named

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:47.280
Arrhenius, um, who was awarded the
Nobel prize back in 1903, Um, for this

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:51.290
is that he was examining the properties
of different aqueous solutions.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:55.329
So meaning different types of
chemicals that were dissolved in water.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.149
And the one thing that he
noticed was that acids, acids

00:00:59.189 --> 00:01:05.020
are substances that dissociate or
break apart when they are in water.

00:01:05.419 --> 00:01:11.660
Now, when those things break, they
tend to produce H plus in water, which

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:13.330
is one of the most important parts.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:19.539
So being that it produces, it produces
H plus, uh, that denotes an acid and

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:23.870
a base is something that dissociated
and produced hydroxide or OH minus.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.000
Now, one of the things that you
generally learn in your chemistry course

00:01:28.009 --> 00:01:31.850
is when you're naming acids, you're
always looking for an H plus at the

00:01:31.880 --> 00:01:36.509
beginning of the formula, whereas a
base, you usually traditionally learn

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.690
that you have an OH minus at the end.

00:01:39.259 --> 00:01:43.979
Now, being that we're talking about ions,
well, this all relates to electrolytes.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:49.390
So all of these different acids and
bases can be classified as electrolytes,

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:54.400
both strong and weak electrolytes,
because when they dissolve, they are

00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:58.699
ionized, meaning they can conduct
electricity as a result, right?

00:01:58.710 --> 00:02:02.159
A lot of the batteries that you have
in your different devices generally

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:04.129
have acid base characteristics.

00:02:04.544 --> 00:02:06.844
If you ever look at your car
battery under your hood, you might

00:02:06.844 --> 00:02:09.984
notice that it says that it's corro
that it's very corrosive on it.

00:02:09.985 --> 00:02:13.645
And then it might even say on the
inside of it, there's sulfuric acid.

00:02:14.095 --> 00:02:17.274
Same thing if you ever have like
a, let's say a battery in the

00:02:17.274 --> 00:02:20.524
back of like a device, one of the
removable ones, you might see that

00:02:20.524 --> 00:02:22.334
it might get battery acid on it.

00:02:22.334 --> 00:02:25.925
And that's telling you, Hey, this is
leaking out and that it is corroding.

00:02:26.679 --> 00:02:30.959
Now, when it comes to electrolytes,
the whole idea is that essentially what

00:02:30.959 --> 00:02:35.799
we are doing is we're taking something
like, let's say HCl, which is an acid.

00:02:36.219 --> 00:02:41.409
And if we are to, let's say, throw
that into water, it will dissociate.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.879
So the way that we would write
that, it was, we would say, Hey,

00:02:43.879 --> 00:02:46.470
we have HCl, this is aqueous.

00:02:47.030 --> 00:02:52.239
We would draw a dissociation reaction,
just saying that they are breaking apart.

00:02:52.705 --> 00:02:57.645
in water and we are going to
be making H plus and Cl minus.

00:02:57.665 --> 00:03:02.014
And this is all going off of
solubility rules when it comes to

00:03:02.014 --> 00:03:03.574
those different acids and bases.

00:03:04.095 --> 00:03:09.345
So being that it generated H plus that's
being surrounded by waters, this tells

00:03:09.345 --> 00:03:12.894
you this made H plus that increased.

00:03:13.164 --> 00:03:18.385
So this is an acid based on
the Arrhenius acid definition.

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:24.330
Now, from there, if I go to an Arrhenius
base, for example, with sodium hydroxide,

00:03:24.765 --> 00:03:26.484
Well, it's the same general idea.

00:03:26.534 --> 00:03:31.024
If I were to throw sodium hydroxide
in water, well, what's going to happen

00:03:31.285 --> 00:03:36.375
is that it's going to dissociate
or break into its individual ions,

00:03:36.674 --> 00:03:40.334
which are Na plus and OH minus.

00:03:40.415 --> 00:03:43.654
And they are both going
to be aqueous as a result.

00:03:43.974 --> 00:03:47.774
They will be solvated by water,
which will cause them to dissociate.

00:03:48.234 --> 00:03:52.015
And this is what we refer to when we
talk about different electrolytes.

00:03:52.355 --> 00:03:56.365
In solution, let's take a look at some
different types of chemical reactions

00:03:56.365 --> 00:04:01.095
that acids and bases are capable of doing
and some other definitions behind this.

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:12.375
Acids and bases are generally responsible
for some of the most, some of the most

00:04:12.375 --> 00:04:16.284
dangerous and some of the most beautiful
reactions that we can see in science.

00:04:16.725 --> 00:04:20.125
Um, many of them have to do
by how you work biologically.

00:04:20.555 --> 00:04:25.395
Um, but one of the most common ones is
neutralization, meaning that when you

00:04:25.424 --> 00:04:27.945
add an acid and you mix it with a base.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:33.150
Uh, what is going to be happening is
the hydrogen ion from the hpl, from

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:38.860
the acid from h plus and hydroxide,
OH minus from the base, they will

00:04:38.860 --> 00:04:42.220
combine and neutralize to form water.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.340
Now the other part of the reaction
is the cat ion in the ion in

00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:48.810
the reaction will make a salt.

00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:52.900
So every time you neutralize
something, you make a salt.

00:04:53.340 --> 00:04:54.369
and water.

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:58.539
So if I were to go, if I was going
to be adding, let's say HCl or

00:04:58.540 --> 00:05:02.990
hydrochloric acid to sodium hydroxide,
well, what are we going to be making?

00:05:03.000 --> 00:05:08.030
We're going to make a water and salt
because this is a double displacement

00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:11.669
reaction inside bonds with outside, right?

00:05:11.669 --> 00:05:15.520
The inside bonds together
making a salt or NACL, right?

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:16.830
Cation is always first.

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:21.780
And then the outside bonds
together, making H2O, or water.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:26.420
So this is the way that we would
generally define an acid base reaction.

00:05:26.729 --> 00:05:30.769
Now this might be one specific
illustration of this, but we

00:05:30.769 --> 00:05:33.890
can kind of follow the general
definition for acids and bases.

00:05:34.690 --> 00:05:39.539
It's common to see where we'll use, put
an H out front to represent an acid.

00:05:40.225 --> 00:05:45.535
The X represents the anion or the
negative ion that's connected to and

00:05:45.535 --> 00:05:49.145
that's going to be reacting with a
base that has a hydroxide component.

00:05:49.565 --> 00:05:51.334
We'll trade the different pieces.

00:05:51.730 --> 00:05:55.430
to make water and salt as a product.

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:59.040
So this is one of the major
types of reactions when it

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.410
comes to acid based chemistry.

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:02.789
Let's look at some more definitions.

00:06:02.850 --> 00:06:06.000
Definition was not a
complete picture, right?

00:06:06.049 --> 00:06:09.659
H plus is generally the way that
we do describe acids, especially

00:06:09.659 --> 00:06:11.769
when we work with the pH scale.

00:06:12.129 --> 00:06:14.119
However, it was slightly incomplete.

00:06:14.690 --> 00:06:18.280
The thing about acids and
bases is that they only explain

00:06:18.650 --> 00:06:23.220
different types of molecules
that contain H plus and OH minus.

00:06:23.795 --> 00:06:25.915
meaning this definition is very limited.

00:06:25.925 --> 00:06:28.235
There's other different types of acid.

00:06:28.255 --> 00:06:30.875
There's different types
of bases specifically that

00:06:30.925 --> 00:06:32.695
don't follow this definition.

00:06:33.084 --> 00:06:37.434
So in order to explain this better in
terms of solution, especially for a

00:06:37.434 --> 00:06:42.060
molecule like ammonia, which is one
of the most common weak bases that

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.590
we, that we are exposed to generally.

00:06:44.989 --> 00:06:47.160
Um, it doesn't follow this definition.

00:06:47.180 --> 00:06:48.530
It doesn't donate a proton.

00:06:48.630 --> 00:06:51.449
However, what it can do is
it can receive one, right?

00:06:51.449 --> 00:06:55.780
It doesn't generate H plus or generate
OH minus, but it can accept one.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:00.350
So what this definition instead of the
other one, so two different definitions.

00:07:00.979 --> 00:07:05.309
So Arrhenius acid bases were
increasing H plus and OH minus.

00:07:05.990 --> 00:07:13.040
The Bronsted Lowry definition is that
an acid will donate a proton, and a base

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:18.570
will accept or receive that proton, so
it's all about giving and receiving.

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:23.730
So whenever we have an acid, right, it
leads with H and what it's trying to do,

00:07:23.730 --> 00:07:25.530
it's kind of start trying to play catch.

00:07:26.395 --> 00:07:28.054
Give that proton away.

00:07:28.635 --> 00:07:31.914
So whenever we have that, one
thing to kind of remind ourselves

00:07:31.914 --> 00:07:33.624
is, well, what is H plus?

00:07:34.095 --> 00:07:36.544
This is what we would call a proton.

00:07:36.655 --> 00:07:38.335
Now, why is that the case?

00:07:38.365 --> 00:07:39.645
Why do we call it a proton?

00:07:39.724 --> 00:07:43.484
Well, you got to think back to
the base from long time ago with

00:07:43.515 --> 00:07:47.435
regular basic chemistry and it all
had to do with, well, if you have a

00:07:47.465 --> 00:07:52.425
neutral hydrogen atom, it contained
one proton and one electron, right?

00:07:52.425 --> 00:07:55.734
And it's spot number one on the periodic
table, meaning one proton, one electron.

00:07:56.075 --> 00:07:56.844
one electron.

00:07:57.594 --> 00:08:03.335
However, when it's H plus, that
means it loses that one electron.

00:08:03.775 --> 00:08:04.805
So what is left?

00:08:05.155 --> 00:08:10.924
If it lost the one electron, the only
thing left is that one single proton.

00:08:11.044 --> 00:08:13.895
And that's why we call an H plus a proton.

00:08:14.664 --> 00:08:19.355
So whenever we are talking about a
bronze, that Lowry acid, we talk, we

00:08:19.355 --> 00:08:22.255
refer to that as a proton donor, right?

00:08:22.255 --> 00:08:25.174
So it received, or so
it's donating that H plus.

00:08:25.685 --> 00:08:29.185
Whereas a base is a proton
acceptor, all right?

00:08:29.185 --> 00:08:32.255
So that's one of the common
ways that we describe this.

00:08:32.505 --> 00:08:37.835
Let's look at some examples of trying
to label proton donors and acceptors.

00:08:37.955 --> 00:08:41.894
With all this, the whole idea to
label an acid and base is figuring out

00:08:41.894 --> 00:08:43.714
what will donate a proton to a base.

00:08:44.025 --> 00:08:45.735
what will receive one.

00:08:46.185 --> 00:08:49.165
So the easiest way is to find the acid.

00:08:49.515 --> 00:08:55.304
Acids always have H leading the
formula when it comes to that molecule.

00:08:55.625 --> 00:08:58.055
Um, so for example, I
have hydrochloric acid.

00:08:58.105 --> 00:08:59.605
That was a strong acid.

00:08:59.904 --> 00:09:02.605
I always recommend memorizing
your strong acids because it will

00:09:02.605 --> 00:09:04.005
save you on a lot of questions.

00:09:04.465 --> 00:09:09.515
So this one, what it's going to do
is because this is our acid, It will

00:09:09.645 --> 00:09:14.234
donate its H plus to NH3 or ammonia.

00:09:14.535 --> 00:09:15.744
Ammonia is a base.

00:09:16.074 --> 00:09:20.355
Even though it has hydrogens on it, that
does not make it automatically a acid.

00:09:20.355 --> 00:09:23.454
It has to have an acid at
the beginning of the formula.

00:09:24.204 --> 00:09:28.925
So when that happens, well, what will
be left of HCl if it loses its proton?

00:09:29.335 --> 00:09:33.435
Well, the only thing left is
going to be Cl minus remaining.

00:09:34.075 --> 00:09:37.835
And then on the other side,
the NH3 is going to receive

00:09:37.875 --> 00:09:42.275
a proton And become NH4 plus.

00:09:42.475 --> 00:09:47.135
So we always have a plus and a minus
between these two different pieces.

00:09:47.605 --> 00:09:52.814
So the acid donated its proton to
the base and the base accepted one.

00:09:54.344 --> 00:09:58.275
If we continue to look at this, so
let's say if I do another one, well,

00:09:58.285 --> 00:10:00.155
once again, we have HCl and water.

00:10:00.505 --> 00:10:01.445
Water is unique.

00:10:01.515 --> 00:10:05.805
Water can actually behave as
either an acid or a base completely

00:10:05.805 --> 00:10:07.915
depending on what is it reacting with.

00:10:07.965 --> 00:10:10.445
What is the strength of
what it is reacting with.

00:10:10.905 --> 00:10:15.305
So if the other component, like HCL L
is really strong, then at then the acid

00:10:15.305 --> 00:10:17.255
is gonna be its acid just like before.

00:10:17.945 --> 00:10:19.055
So HCL is our acid.

00:10:19.205 --> 00:10:24.495
So what it's gonna do is it's going
to donate, its proton to water.

00:10:25.105 --> 00:10:27.415
So what's gonna be left of HCL L?

00:10:27.535 --> 00:10:32.815
Well, we got rid of an H plus, so
the only thing left is CL minus.

00:10:33.695 --> 00:10:37.565
And the reason it's CL minus is being
that we left an electron behind.

00:10:38.055 --> 00:10:40.875
That means chlorine
has one extra electron.

00:10:41.265 --> 00:10:45.755
So it might kind of help to see how
that entire Lewis structure looks like.

00:10:46.195 --> 00:10:48.675
So kind of reminding you
of a really long time ago.

00:10:49.095 --> 00:10:55.265
So if we have, let's say water and we
have the structure of HCl, well, that

00:10:55.275 --> 00:11:01.339
proton is going to get transferred
And put on to the other water and as

00:11:01.339 --> 00:11:06.470
a result, it's going to have an extra,
extra hydrogen and then chlorine is

00:11:06.480 --> 00:11:08.220
going to have its negative charge.

00:11:08.759 --> 00:11:16.199
So we'll have chlorine and then H3O
plus, which is known as hydronium.

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:21.310
So this is the hydronium ion and
then we have the chloride ion.

00:11:22.849 --> 00:11:23.359
Okay.

00:11:23.529 --> 00:11:24.859
So that's our acid.

00:11:25.315 --> 00:11:25.915
and our base.

00:11:26.235 --> 00:11:27.305
Let's look at one more.

00:11:28.005 --> 00:11:30.205
So right now I have ammonia and water.

00:11:30.885 --> 00:11:33.525
So ammonia is a very common base.

00:11:33.575 --> 00:11:36.665
So that is one thing you want
to kind of keep in your head is

00:11:36.665 --> 00:11:38.914
that ammonia is typically a base.

00:11:39.225 --> 00:11:43.715
And like I said before, water can
act as either an acid or a base

00:11:44.135 --> 00:11:46.025
depends on what it's interacting with.

00:11:46.575 --> 00:11:49.675
If the other components are
stronger acid, then water is a base.

00:11:50.094 --> 00:11:53.064
If the other component is a stronger
base, then water is an acid.

00:11:53.145 --> 00:11:55.954
It's a little confusing, but
it will, you'll come over time.

00:11:56.845 --> 00:12:00.365
So if water acts like an acid,
it's going to donate its proton.

00:12:00.895 --> 00:12:02.054
So what is left?

00:12:02.385 --> 00:12:07.335
Well, the only thing remaining is
going to be OH and OH8, and it'll be a

00:12:07.345 --> 00:12:10.175
minus sign because we lost an H plus.

00:12:11.004 --> 00:12:14.954
On the other side, we're
going to have NH4 plus.

00:12:15.680 --> 00:12:22.000
For ammonium ion, ammonium and hydroxide.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:29.240
So not only is this a, so if you kind of
look at this one particularly, so this, as

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:33.140
you saw ammonia is a Bronsted Lowry base.

00:12:33.660 --> 00:12:38.360
Our Bronsted base and you can also see
that it produced hydroxide as a result.

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:42.560
So it also could be defined as something
that when it reacts with water.

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:45.050
So water could be defined as
something that could actually

00:12:45.060 --> 00:12:47.050
