00:15:25.234 --> 00:15:27.615
Um, the other big thing that
happens as well is that they

00:15:27.615 --> 00:15:31.224
react with a lot of carbonates
and bicarbonates to produce CO2.

00:15:32.375 --> 00:15:35.205
So one of the big things that's
in soda is that you'll see that

00:15:35.205 --> 00:15:36.165
they're on the ingredients.

00:15:36.215 --> 00:15:37.505
There's carbonic acid.

00:15:37.910 --> 00:15:42.709
Well, what carbonic acid does is
it's a gas evolution chemical.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:49.290
So it will actually break down
into water and CO2, which is where

00:15:49.300 --> 00:15:52.699
all that fizz and bubbles come
from, from carbonated beverages.

00:15:53.605 --> 00:15:55.525
Let's look at the properties of bases.

00:15:55.535 --> 00:15:59.345
So with the properties of bases,
these ones are tend to be very bitter.

00:15:59.375 --> 00:16:02.855
They have a soapy feeling to them,
meaning that they're slippery.

00:16:03.015 --> 00:16:06.475
They can even are described
as tasting soapy or chalky.

00:16:06.874 --> 00:16:10.395
Um, one example of that chalky
taste is if you ever have a Tums,

00:16:10.544 --> 00:16:12.704
which is an anti acid or antacid.

00:16:13.870 --> 00:16:17.260
That will react with, uh, essentially
an acid, neutralize it when you,

00:16:17.260 --> 00:16:19.880
if you have a really upset stomach,
but they usually taste chalky.

00:16:20.569 --> 00:16:24.839
Um, other one, other things is that they
change color of the litmus paper as well.

00:16:25.250 --> 00:16:29.269
So if you take, uh, let's say a red piece
of litmus paper, so there's two colors.

00:16:29.655 --> 00:16:31.935
And you dip it into a
base, it will change blue.

00:16:32.625 --> 00:16:35.315
Um, the other thing is that they
typically feel very slippery.

00:16:35.325 --> 00:16:38.975
Now keep in mind with acids and bases,
you don't want to start tasting all

00:16:38.975 --> 00:16:40.094
these different chemicals, right?

00:16:40.415 --> 00:16:43.985
That was something I did in the early
1800s and 1900s and most of them didn't

00:16:43.995 --> 00:16:45.674
really last super long afterwards.

00:16:45.674 --> 00:16:46.805
That's not a safe thing to do.

00:16:47.384 --> 00:16:50.064
Um, but if you do get some on your
skin, you feel it's all of a sudden

00:16:50.074 --> 00:16:53.995
really slippery on your skin, wash your
hands for, for as long as to get those

00:16:54.015 --> 00:16:55.555
chemicals off to keep yourself safe.

00:16:56.225 --> 00:17:00.204
And then the last thing is they also
conduct electricity because they are.

00:17:00.545 --> 00:17:01.735
Electrolytes, right?

00:17:01.875 --> 00:17:04.774
So a strong electrolyte will
conduct a lot of electricity.

00:17:05.224 --> 00:17:08.915
Um, whereas a weak electrolyte, not so
much because it does not dissociate fully.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:11.180
Let's look at all of our definitions.

00:17:11.319 --> 00:17:15.040
So when it comes to acids and bases,
there are three major definitions.

00:17:15.040 --> 00:17:17.359
We went over two of them in this video.

00:17:18.000 --> 00:17:20.969
The first one is the
Arrhenius acid and base.

00:17:21.050 --> 00:17:25.040
This is essentially your, your
bare bones basic idea of how

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:26.310
to describe an acid and base.

00:17:26.660 --> 00:17:31.250
This is usually what we use to
describe the pH or the p o h of

00:17:31.260 --> 00:17:33.840
different solutions, something
you'll learn about in other videos.

00:17:34.785 --> 00:17:38.785
So, whenever you have an Arrhenius acid,
it increases the proton concentration.

00:17:39.455 --> 00:17:43.555
When you have an Arrhenius
base, it increases the hydroxide

00:17:43.685 --> 00:17:45.455
concentration as a result.

00:17:46.635 --> 00:17:50.744
When it comes to, let's say, the
Bronsted Lowry definition, what's

00:17:50.744 --> 00:17:58.375
happening is that the acid is going to
be donating its H proton, to the base.

00:17:58.475 --> 00:17:59.375
So, you're going to see
that getting increased.

00:18:00.050 --> 00:18:02.459
traded right from one to the other.

00:18:03.320 --> 00:18:08.540
The last one, which is not in this video,
but is very common in organic chemistry.

00:18:08.889 --> 00:18:12.549
And something to be aware of if you
do have to go into Chem 1A, 1B in

00:18:12.550 --> 00:18:17.149
organic, or if you're even in some of
the biological sciences, you might see

00:18:17.149 --> 00:18:22.510
them is that there's another one called
Lewis acids and bases named after the

00:18:22.510 --> 00:18:24.630
same scientists that did Lewis diagrams.

00:18:25.270 --> 00:18:29.550
So the whole idea here is that essentially
it's all about electrons moving.

00:18:30.120 --> 00:18:35.580
So a base typically donates electrons
and acid receives electrons, but this

00:18:35.580 --> 00:18:39.659
is not in my course for introductory
chemistry, but I did want you to

00:18:39.659 --> 00:18:42.480
know about the definition in case
you come across it and any of the

00:18:42.489 --> 00:18:45.149
materials that you read about or study.

00:18:45.680 --> 00:18:45.990
All right.

00:18:46.130 --> 00:18:49.210
So these are your three definitions
in terms of acids and bases.

00:18:49.625 --> 00:18:53.215
You do have to understand the idea
of Arrhenius, acids and bases and

00:18:53.215 --> 00:18:55.065
the Bronsted Lowry acids and bases.

